 Multiline strings: `key word`, then the lines under it, one tab deeper and verbatim from there. Blank lines inside are kept.
query sql
	SELECT name, age
	FROM users

	WHERE age > 30
		AND name LIKE 'a%'


 An empty tag is plain text, and so is txt. A shebang inside is just text
script 
	#!/bin/bash
	echo "hello world"
notes txt
	just text

 Comma keys share the string. A one-word value with nothing under it is an ordinary leaf
a,b txt
	shared
dialect sql
after 1

 Nested: strings work at any depth, a comment may follow the tag, and tabs inside are text
config	db
	init sql	 schema
		CREATE TABLE t (
			id INT
		);
	port 5432
table txt
	name	age
	ryan	30
